config AVR_AVRDX_WS2812_SHORT_LANE
	bool "Limit number of LEDs in lane for faster execution"
	default false
	depends on AVR_AVRDX_DRIVER_WS2812
	---help---
		When the LED data is submitted by the application, the bits
		are converted to whatever data the hardware needs to be able
		to transmit those bits. Generally, that means using more
		memory - for example, the UART lanes convert each 3 bytes
		of LED data into 8 + 1 bytes of UART data (more if the LEDs
		have white component.) Walking through this data needs
		an iterator variable (index) - data type needed for this
		variable is determined by LED count.

		If you select this option, the LED count is limited to make
		uint8_t large enough to hold the index. The limitation is
		31 LEDs without support for white LED (see WS2812_HAS_WHITE)
		or 21 LEDs with it.

		If you do not select it, the LED count is limited to a hardcoded
		limit of 255 LEDs (per lane.)

config AVR_AVRDX_WS2812_NO_READS
	bool "Disable reading the pixel buffer"
	default false
	depends on AVR_AVRDX_DRIVER_WS2812
	---help---
		The upper half driver expects to be able to read data present
		in the pixel buffer. That means that the lower half driver
		needs to store what it receives in write call.

		The data is, however, also stored in the form suitable
		for transmission by the hardware. In essence, everything
		is stored twice.

		If your application never reads data from the pixel buffer,
		you can enable this option. Any attempt to read will return
		ENOSYS and the pixel buffer will not be allocated.

config AVR_AVRDX_WS2812_NO_WHITE_LED
	bool "Suppress warning for white LED"
	depends on AVR_AVRDX_DRIVER_WS2812
	depends on WS2812_HAS_WHITE
	default false
	---help---
		This driver implements support for LEDs with white component
		but the author of the code did not have any such device
		to test. Therefore, support for these LEDs is disabled
		and the compiler will issue a warning. Any attempt to register
		LED with has_white set will return an error.

		It is preferred to disable WS2812_HAS_WHITE altogether but
		if you need it on (for example because the system has some other
		WS2812 device using different lower-half), you can use this
		option to suppress the compile-time warning and reduce
		code size.

		The code will be compiled as if WS2812_HAS_WHITE was unset.
